WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.078
Your class is going to build one fingerboard skate park. Each team of three designs two required sections, plus up to two stretch sections if you’re on track.

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:15.186
Ramps, ledges, rails, stairs: whatever you dream up.

00:00:15.536 --> 00:00:20.958
Every section uses the same class connector and clearance, so they all snap together.

00:00:21.308 --> 00:00:26.475
Each section must fit within 180 millimeters, at real fingerboard scale.

00:00:27.775 --> 00:00:32.987
In Week 16, you plan and design. Your park plan is due Thursday, May 6.

00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:42.591
In Week 17, you model and check. Send your first section by Tuesday, May 11. All required print files are due Thursday, May 13.

00:00:42.941 --> 00:00:48.235
In Week 18, licensed students print, and you fit-test and revise.

00:00:48.585 --> 00:00:55.806
In Week 19, you ride-test across sections and revise again. Last reprints go in by May 27.

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:59.860
In Week 20, you assemble the whole park and show it off.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:11.226
Before you submit print files, run the pre-flight check. Fits the plate. Uses the standard. Prints flat. Nothing too thin. Millimeters. Named right.

00:01:11.576 --> 00:01:16.580
Required sections print first. Stretch sections print only if there’s printer time.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:24.172
When sections come back, fit them together and save a named version in Onshape for each revision.

00:01:24.522 --> 00:01:25.718
Then ride the park.

00:01:26.068 --> 00:01:30.190
Every teammate presents their section. Twelve teams, one park.
